cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you refer to this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Scaffale: local indexing, OCR and semantic search of a personal research archive with a local language model"
version: "1.1"
abstract: "Scaffale is a first attempt to bring large language models into a historian's everyday work without leaving the computer. It takes a disordered research folder (PDFs, Word files, notes, spreadsheets, old databases, scans, folders of photographed pages) and turns it into a searchable archive: exact, thesaurus-based and semantic search (bge-m3 embeddings), every passage linked to the right page of the original, questions to the documents answered only from the archive's own passages with numbered sources and a check of every number, summaries, themes, duplicates and a list of what is still missing. PDFs and scans become checked Markdown transcriptions: every line with numbers or dates is read by independent readers, a third reader acts as arbiter, and unconfirmed lines are flagged for a check on the original. Everything runs on the local computer with open models installed through Ollama and llama.cpp; no document is uploaded. Two configurations were built and measured: an ordinary Windows 11 laptop with no dedicated graphics card (Tesseract, glm-ocr and PaddleOCR as readers, qwen3:8b for answers) and a Linux research workstation with two 96 GB GPUs, where Qwen3.5-397B-A17B reads the pages and Surya OCR 2.5 is the arbiter. Developed for the author's historical research archive (about 15,000 documents), it made possible the corpus-wide study of the sources on the Madonna Nicopeia, and in the author's doctoral research it is presented as a first working example of a large language model installed and run locally on an ordinary Windows PC, applied to the semantic analysis of archives, including historical ones."
authors:
